The `filter()` method in JavaScript creates a new array containing only elements that pass a test implemented by a callback function:

```javascript
// Basic example: filter even numbers
const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];
const evens = numbers.filter(num => num % 2 === 0);
console.log(evens); // [2, 4, 6]

// Filter objects by property
const users = [
  { name: 'Alice', age: 17 },
  { name: 'Bob', age: 22 },
  { name: 'Charlie', age: 19 },
  { name: 'Diana', age: 15 }
];
const adults = users.filter(user => user.age >= 18);
console.log(adults); // [{ name: 'Bob', age: 22 }, { name: 'Charlie', age: 19 }]

// Filter strings by length
const words = ['apple', 'hi', 'banana', 'cat', 'date'];
const longWords = words.filter(word => word.length > 3);
console.log(longWords); // ['apple', 'banana', 'date']

// Filter with index
const everyOther = numbers.filter((num, index) => index % 2 === 0);
console.log(everyOther); // [1, 3, 5]

// Chaining filter with map
const result = numbers
  .filter(n => n > 2)
  .map(n => n * 10);
console.log(result); // [30, 40, 50, 60]
```

The callback receives three arguments: the current element, the index, and the original array. `filter()` does not modify the original array. Combine it with `map()` and `reduce()` for powerful data transformation pipelines.
